      "problem": "What is $\\dfrac {x^2 + x - 6} {x-2}$ reduced to lowest terms?",
      "worked_solution": "The numerator can be factored as $x^2+x-6= (x+3)(x-2).$ Cancelling out any common factors that appear in the numerator and the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-6}{x-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)(x-2)}{x-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)\\cancel{(x-2)}}{\\cancel{(x-2)}} & = \\\\ \\frac{x+3}{1} & = \\\\ x+3 & .\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
